{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778979132211,"sessionID":"ses_1cc94d36effeOhlnHzaXDGcEx2","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_iotAKLnN54b2TBIBSqTnFsnM","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json","offset":1,"limit":400},"output":"<path>/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\n2:   \"body\": \"Please check that the term does not already exist by using the ontology search tool OLS:\\nhttps://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl\\n\\n\\n**Preferred term label**\\nhybrid osteochondral skeletal cell \\n\\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\\n\\n\\n**Definition** (free text, with reference(s), please. PubMed ID format is PMID:XXXXXX)\\n\\nA skeletal cell at the periosteal surface of the murine rib that displays hybrid osteochondral properties, emerging within the large callus that bridges segmental rib defects. It derives from a Sox9-expressing periosteal skeletal stem/progenitor subpopulation that constitutes only a small fraction of uninjured rib periosteum. After injury, these cells populate the callus and co-express cartilage and bone regulators Sox9 and Runx2, as well as matrix genes Col2a1 and Col1a1, while exhibiting dual chondrocyte/osteoblast properties.\\n\\nPMID: [30983567](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30983567/)\\n\\n**Parent cell type term** (check the hierarchy here https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl)\\nskeletal cell\\n\\n**Anatomical structure where the cell type is found** (check Uberon for anatomical structures: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/uberon)\\nperiosteum\\n\\n**Your ORCID**\\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277\\n\\n**Additional notes or concerns**\\nThis cell has been identified in mice \\n\\n\",\n3:   \"comments\": [],\n4:   \"title\": \"[NTR] hybrid osteochondral skeletal cell\",\n5:   \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3534\"\n6: }\n\n(End of file - total 6 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\n  \"body\": \"Please check that the term does not already exist by using the ontology search tool OLS:\\nhttps://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl\\n\\n\\n**Preferred term label**\\nhybrid osteochondral skeletal cell \\n\\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\\n\\n\\n**Definition** (free text, with reference(s), please. PubMed ID format is PMID:XXXXXX)\\n\\nA skeletal cell at the periosteal surface of the murine rib that displays hybrid osteochondral properties, emerging within the large callus that bridges segmental rib defects. It derives from a Sox9-expressing periosteal skeletal stem/progenitor subpopulation that constitutes only a small fraction of uninjured rib periosteum. After injury, these cells populate the callus and co-express cartilage and bone regulators Sox9 and Runx2, as well as matrix genes Col2a1 and Col1a1, while exhibiting dual chondrocyte/osteoblast properties.\\n\\nPMID: [30983567](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30983567/)\\n\\n**Parent cell type term** (check the hierarchy here https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl)\\nskeletal cell\\n\\n**Anatomical structure where the cell type is found** (check Uberon for anatomical structures: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/uberon)\\nperiosteum\\n\\n**Your ORCID**\\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277\\n\\n**Additional notes or concerns**\\nThis cell has been identified in mice \\n\\n\",\n  \"comments\": [],\n  \"title\": \"[NTR] hybrid osteochondral skeletal cell\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3534\"\n}","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"__issue_context__.json","time":{"start":1778979132114,"end":1778979132208}},"id":"prt_e336b52c6001L66dn8SJpLYNP2","sessionID":"ses_1cc94d36effeOhlnHzaXDGcEx2","messageID":"msg_e336b44ae001lF1I42O4ODP9mH"}}
